      "cool_grays_browns_irregular_pattern_mo": "Cool grays + browns, irregular pattern. Moderate everything — the chameleon of cephalopod scenes.",
      "cooling_sea_level_drop_wipe_out_85_of_": "Cooling + sea-level drop wipe out ~85% of marine species. Nautiloid cephalopods are decimated but survive.",
      "cooperative_hunt_opportunity_a_grouper": "Cooperative hunt opportunity — a grouper signals interest",
      "coral_bleaching_events_have_occurred_g": "Coral bleaching events have occurred globally in 1998, 2002, 2010, 2014-17, 2020, and 2023-24 — each killing 20-50% of affected reefs. Reef-dwelling octopuses lose habitat.",
      "coral_reef": "Coral reef",
      "coral_reef_fishes_revised_edition": "Coral Reef Fishes (revised edition)",
      "coral_reef_habitat_5_30m_most_octopuse": "Coral reef habitat, 5-30m. Most octopuses live here.",
      "coral_rubble": "Coral Rubble",
      "coral_rubble_accumulates_after_storm_c": "Coral rubble accumulates after storm + cyclone damage. Increasing under climate change. Can become permanent habitat if colonized by encrusting organisms.",
      "coral_spawning": "Coral Spawning",
      "correct_1_atm_per_10_m_of_depth_so_300": "Correct! 1 atm per 10 m of depth, so 3000 m / 10 = 300 atm at depth.",
      "correct_500_million_neurons_young_1971": "Correct. ~500 million neurons (Young 1971 estimates) — but 2/3 are in the arms, not the central brain. This is comparable to a small dog.",
      "correct_800_living_species_are_describ": "Correct. ~800 living species are described, but the true number is likely higher — deep-sea species are still being discovered, and several \"species complexes\" are being split into multiple species via genetic analysis.",
      "correct_all_cephalopods_are_ectothermi": "Correct. All cephalopods are ectothermic; their metabolic rates rise with water temperature, which is why warming oceans accelerate cephalopod lifecycles.",
      "correct_allen_et_al_2010_documented_ca": "Correct. Allen et al. 2010 documented camouflage transitions in < 200 ms — faster than a human eyeblink.",
      "correct_boyle_rodhouse_2005_document_9": "Correct. Boyle & Rodhouse 2005 document >99% paralarval mortality — most cephalopod eggs that hatch never reach reproductive age. This bottleneck is the major regulator of cephalopod populations.",
      "correct_broadly_accepted_by_comparativ": "Correct — broadly accepted by comparative psychologists. Mather, Hanlon, Fiorito, and many others document cephalopod cognition exceeding any other invertebrate group.",
      "correct_cephalopoda_is_the_class_withi": "Correct. Cephalopoda is the class within phylum Mollusca that contains all cephalopods. The name means \"head-foot\" in Greek.",
      "correct_chromatophore_patterns_arm_pos": "Correct. Chromatophore patterns + arm positions are the primary signaling system. Especially elaborate in cuttlefish during mating.",
      "correct_derby_2014_documented_that_ink": "Correct. Derby 2014 documented that ink chemistry blinds AND smells — pleasing predators that hunt by smell (sharks, eels) AS WELL as visual hunters.",
      "correct_doubleday_et_al_2016_curr_biol": "Correct. Doubleday et al. 2016 (Curr. Biol.) documented globally increasing cephalopod biomass since 1950 — likely due to faster life cycles + warmer water suiting them while slow-growing fish predators decline.",
      "correct_eight_is_the_defining_feature_": "Correct. Eight is the defining feature (\"octo-\" prefix means eight).",
      "correct_finn_tregenza_norman_2009_docu": "Correct — Finn, Tregenza & Norman, 2009 documented this behavior in Indonesia and called it the first invertebrate tool use of its kind.",
      "correct_freitas_et_al_2022_found_75_of": "Correct — Freitas et al. 2022 found 75% of observed individuals used plastic, glass, or metal debris as shelter.",
      "correct_hemocyanin_uses_copper_instead": "Correct. Hemocyanin uses copper instead of iron; oxidized copper is blue.",
      "correct_humboldt_squid_market_squid_ca": "Correct. Humboldt squid + market squid can sprint at ~25 km/h via jet propulsion. Flying squid can briefly LEAP from water at ~36 km/h.",
      "correct_iglesias_et_al_2019_j_exp_biol": "Correct — Iglesias et al. 2019 (J. Exp. Biol.) and Medeiros et al. 2021 (iScience) documented REM-like state changes with chromatophore activity in sleeping octopuses.",
      "correct_iridophores_use_stacks_of_refl": "Correct. Iridophores use stacks of reflectin protein platelets to make BLUE and GREEN via thin-film interference — structural color, not pigment color.",
      "correct_most_cephalopods_complete_a_ge": "Correct. Most cephalopods complete a generation in 1-2 years, so population responses are far faster than for long-lived fish. This makes them BOTH vulnerable AND useful as ecosystem-change indicators.",
      "correct_most_coastal_octopuses_are_sho": "Correct. Most coastal octopuses are short-lived. Senescence after reproduction is the main killer.",
      "correct_nautilus_pompilius_relatives_w": "Correct — Nautilus pompilius + relatives were CITES Appendix II listed in 2017 because their slow reproduction (1 egg every 6-12 months) makes them vulnerable to shell harvesting.",
      "correct_nautiluses_see_using_a_primiti": "Correct. Nautiluses see using a primitive pinhole-camera eye — no lens, only a small aperture. This is evolutionarily ancient and reflects a slower diversification of vision in this lineage.",
      "correct_o_dor_1988_bartol_2010_jetting": "Correct — O'Dor 1988 + Bartol 2010. Jetting trades efficiency for burst speed. That's why octopuses prefer to crawl and squid that jet routinely have proportionally larger gills.",
      "correct_octopus_and_cuttlefish_pupils_": "Correct. Octopus and cuttlefish pupils contract to a horizontal W or U shape (not a round dot like ours). The Stubbs & Stubbs 2016 hypothesis proposes this shape enables color discrimination via chromatic aberration.",
      "correct_practice_7_is_a_core_argumenta": "Correct. Practice 7 is a core argumentation practice — claim + evidence + reasoning, plus counterclaim engagement.",
      "correct_sea_cucumbers_are_echinoderms_": "Correct! Sea cucumbers are echinoderms, not cephalopods. They lack a head and have radial body plan.",
      "correct_several_companies_nueva_pescan": "Correct. Several companies (Nueva Pescanova in Spain, etc.) are developing octopus farming. Animal-welfare scientists + ethicists (Mather, Sneddon, others) have raised major concerns about whether octopuses can be ethically farmed at scale.",
      "correct_squids_retain_a_reduced_intern": "Correct. Squids retain a reduced internal shell — the gladius. Octopuses have lost it entirely, which is why they can squeeze through tiny gaps.",
      "correct_substrate_type_is_a_primary_ax": "Correct — substrate type is a primary axis of habitat partitioning.",
      "correct_the_pigment_doesn_t_change_the": "Correct — the pigment doesn't change. The CELL gets bigger, exposing more pigment.",
      "correct_this_informed_the_uk_animal_we": "Correct — this informed the UK Animal Welfare (Sentience) Act 2022, which added cephalopods + decapod crustaceans (lobsters, crabs) to the sentience list.",
      "correct_two_distant_lineages_evolved_s": "Correct! Two distant lineages evolved similar solutions to the same problem (focused image-forming vision).",
      "correct_two_specimens_at_the_seattle_a": "Correct. Two specimens at the Seattle Aquarium repeatedly released + grabbed a floating pill bottle in a tank current — a behavior pattern that meets the criteria for animal play (Mather & Anderson 1999, J. Comp. Psychol.).",
      "correct_wells_smith_1992_documented_th": "Correct. Wells & Smith 1992 documented that systemic heart contraction is interrupted during high-pressure mantle contractions — limiting how long an octopus can sustain jet propulsion.",
      "correct_wodinsky_s_experiment_provided": "Correct. Wodinsky's experiment provided the first direct evidence that the optic gland controls the brooding + senescence program. After surgery, females abandoned eggs and resumed feeding.",
      "cortisol_isn_t_the_primary_signaling_s": "Cortisol isn't the primary signaling system here.",
